contentText:
Todd Rundgren has called Taylor Swift the &#8220;apotheosis of mediocrity&#8221; and suggested she &#8220;ruined music&#8221; in a new interview.

Speaking to Vulture , the veteran producer was asked which current artist he would spoof if he made a parody album, a nod to his work on Meat Loaf&#8217;s 1977 album Bat Out of Hell , which he has said he intended partly as a Bruce Springsteen send-up.

&#8220;Taylor Swift, of course. There&#8217;s an argument to be made that she ruined music,&#8221; he said. &#8220;It&#8217;s just that, to me, Taylor Swift is the apotheosis of mediocrity.&#8221;

He went further later in the interview. &#8220;Taylor Swift doesn&#8217;t do anything particularly great except grow hair,&#8221; he said. &#8220;She&#8217;s not a great singer. She&#8217;s not a great songwriter. She&#8217;s not a great dancer.&#8221;

Rundgren praised Lady Gaga as a &#8220;real musician&#8221; and &#8220;classically trained piano player,&#8221; and floated a theory that female pop stars rose to dominance after young men shifted their spending to video games in the 1990s. It isn&#8217;t the first time he&#8217;s criticized Swift — he made similar comments on Rick Rubin&#8217;s Broken Record podcast in 2022.

Swift hasn&#8217;t responded. When The Life of a Showgirl drew mixed reactions on its release last year, she addressed the criticism on Apple Music&#8217;s The Zane Lowe Show .

&#8220;I have a lot of respect for people&#8217;s subjective opinions on art. I&#8217;m not the art police,&#8221; she said. &#8220;Everybody is allowed to feel exactly how they want.&#8221;

The comments arrive days after Swift released The Life of a Showgirl: The Encore , an expanded edition of her 2025 album featuring four new tracks, including “ Patient Zero .” Swift premiered the song’s music video during Sunday’s MTV Video Music Awards 2026 .

The deluxe version of Swift’s 12th studio album, out now, also features the newly recorded song “Babylon,” “Pink Clouding” and “Cleveland!” The original album spent 12 weeks at No. 1 on the Billboard 200 and opened with a record-breaking 4.002 million equivalent album units in the United States in its first week.
